## Proof

Consider the integral
$$I = \int_0^1 (x - \alpha)^2 f(x)\,dx.$$

Expanding and using the three given conditions:
$$I = \int_0^1 x^2 f(x)\,dx - 2\alpha\int_0^1 x f(x)\,dx + \alpha^2\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx = \alpha^2 - 2\alpha\cdot\alpha + \alpha^2\cdot 1 = \alpha^2 - 2\alpha^2 + \alpha^2 = 0.$$

So
$$\int_0^1 (x-\alpha)^2 f(x)\,dx = 0.$$

The integrand $g(x) = (x-\alpha)^2 f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ (since $f$ is continuous), and is **non-negative** everywhere, since $(x-\alpha)^2 \geq 0$ and $f(x) > 0$ by hypothesis. A continuous, non-negative function on a closed interval has integral $0$ only if it is identically zero. Hence
$$(x-\alpha)^2 f(x) = 0 \quad \text{for every } x \in [0,1].$$

Since $f(x) > 0$ for every $x \in [0,1]$, we may divide by $f(x)$ to obtain
$$(x-\alpha)^2 = 0 \quad \text{for every } x \in [0,1],$$
i.e. $x = \alpha$ for every $x \in [0,1]$. This is impossible, since $[0,1]$ contains more than one point.

Therefore no continuous function $f:[0,1]\to(0,\infty)$ satisfies the three given conditions, for any real number $\alpha$.

